  "ref": "JOB.42.7",
  "review_notes": [
    "Correction: t005 *that* returns to the `הָיָה` frame and s013, the apodosis waw, is `unrepresented`. Rule 45 gives the waw the complementizer only where the ASV writes it at the head of the apodosis; here it is written immediately after *And it was so* and ahead of the temporal clause *after Jehovah had spoken*, which carries a subordinator of its own, so the word stands inside the frame and the waw, for which no connective is then written, is `unrepresented` under rule 14. This is the shape of that rule's own example, *And it was so, that when Joshua had spoken unto the people, the seven priests ... passed on*.",
    "The competing reading, which the verse pass took, reads rule 45's source-side sentence alone: the apodosis does open with its own waw, and on that reading the waw carries the *that* while `הָיָה` keeps *it was so*. It is rejected because rule 45's later paragraph turns on where the ASV has written the word, and the word is written ahead of the temporal clause rather than at the apodosis head.",
    "The closing *hath* is read as a second realization of the written `דִבַּרְתֶּם` across the gapped comparative clause (rules 25, 34); the competing reading marks it `added` as a lexical verb supplied for a line whose Hebrew writes none.",
    "`הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה` writes one determiner, which the demonstrative takes, leaving both articles `unrepresented` (rules 41, 47)."
  ]
}
